{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices across the full 30-day horizon (often by ~2–5 EUR, rising to ~4–7 EUR during uptrends). Apply an upward level shift to the entire path.",
    "Trend/lag error: model underestimates sustained rallies and reacts too slowly to regime shifts upward (forecast path stays near recent mean while outcomes trend higher). Add positive drift when the last 1–2 weeks show higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Horizon-dependent widening: underprediction grows with lead time (later days show larger gaps than early days). Use a correction that increases with horizon (e.g., small uplift days 1–5, larger uplift days 10–30).",
    "Volatility compression: forecast range is too narrow versus outcomes (misses amplitude of moves). Inflate deviations from the forecast mean (or add volatility premium) during trending periods.",
    "Post-dip rebound underpricing: after a drawdown in history, the model expects continued weakness/mean reversion down, but realized series rebounds strongly. When the last 30d includes a sharp dip followed by stabilization, bias-correct upward more aggressively."
  ]
}
